Hi,

sorry for the delay on this one. It is seems that you have a special
case that we didn't consider when we designed the naive genotyping
algorithm.  I can have a look at this, but in order to do that I'd
need some example data.  Would you mind making the data for one
tumor-normal pair available for download?  This means the two
*,fracB.asb and the two *,total.asb files in
totalAndFracBData/Retino,ACC,ra,-XY,BPN,-XY,AVG,FLN,-XY/MOUSEDIVm520650/
for this pair.  You can email me the download link offline (not the
mailing list).

/Henrik

On Thu, Mar 15, 2012 at 9:07 AM, Irsan Kooi <e.i....@sms.ed.ac.uk> wrote:
> Dear all,
>
> I am having problems with the callXXorXY method that is used in doing naive
> genotyping as a preparation for TumorBoost. At a certain stage in the naive
> genotyping (see this vignette) you have to infer the gender of your samples
> with the callXXorXY-method. The problem is that based on BAFs from chrX it
> thinks the sample is XY, but based on BAFs from chrY it thinks the sample is
> not XY. I have a theory why this is happening:
>
> As far as I understand it correctly, this method uses findPeaksAndValleys()
> to find out how much peaks there are in a density-plot of BAFs at chrX and
> chrY. If there are two peaks in chrX, it assumes the sample is XY. Then it
> does the same for BAFs at chrY and if the two answers are the same, the
> program continues and otherwise it fails.
>
> This method works perfectly for "normal" samples but when it comes to
> inbreds (so samples that (theoretically) are homozygous at every locus in
> the genome) I think it is causing problems. Assume you have a female inbred
> sample (so XX). Normally, XX-genotypes give rise to three peaks in the
> BAF-density plots: at 0 (for AA), 0.5 (for AB) and at 1 (for BB). But in the
> case of inbreds (where the only possibilities are AA or BB because both
> chromosomes are identical copies) there will be only two peaks: at 0 and at
> 1. Though this sample is truly female, the callXXorXY will think it is male
> because it translates 2 peaks to the sample being XY. At the same time, at
> calls the same female inbred sample XX when it relies on BAFS at chrY
> because there will not be 2 peaks in the BAF-density plot (as there is no
> Y-chromosome in this sample...). This causes the callXXorXY program to
> terminate throwing the following exception:
>
> Error in list(`callXXorXY(betaN[is23], betaN[is24], adjust = adjust, from =
> 0, to = 1)` = <environment>,  :
>
> [2012-03-15 11:37:02] Exception: Allele B fractions for ChrX and ChrY are
> inconsistent.
> ...
>
> What is the best solution to this issue? Setting the gender manually? Or
> make the callXXorXY only use BAF's from chrY to infer the gender (as female
> inbreds do not have Y (so not 2 peaks) but male inbreds to have one copy (so
> 2 peaks) of Y)? Or anything else????
>
> I might be mistaking about some things and I would be happy to hear any
> discussion points about this theory :-)
>
> Irsan
>
>
>
>

